QMD-4.0 — Quadratic Motor Dynamics, Fourth Revision
High-Stability Algebraic Efficiency Model (τ, q, pwm)
Stable. Deterministic. Invertible. Zero-dependency.

1. Overview
QMD-4.0 is the fourth major revision of the Quadratic Motor Dynamics model:
a compact, deterministic motor-efficiency predictor based on three normalized variables:
    • τ — normalized torque factor
    • q — normalized electrical loading / modulation index
    • pwm — normalized switching duty
The model produces a scalar:
eff(τ, q, pwm) = predicted motor efficiency (0–100%).
QMD-4.0 is designed to be:
    • ✔ Algebraic (no lookup tables)
    • ✔ Smooth and differentiable
    • ✔ Fully invertible in τ for a target efficiency
    • ✔ Perfectly stable across the (τ, q, pwm) operating cube
    • ✔ Zero-dependency (C and Python both standalone)
    • ✔ Hardware-oriented, suitable for embedded controllers
QMD-4.0 eliminates all prior irregularities:
    • No ridge-skew
    • No curvature inversions
    • No PWM discontinuities
    • No multimodal peaks
It is the most stable and physically-consistent formulation in the QMD lineage.

2. Why QMD-4.0?
QMD-4.0 introduces a new algebraic core:
eff = B0
    + B_tau2  * (tau - 1)^2
    + B_q     * q
    + B_pwm   * pwm
    + B_cross * q * pwm
Where the curvature term B_tau2 fixes the axial ridge at τ = 1.0 with precision.
This structure produces:
    • The expected efficiency peak at τ = 1.0 for all operating points
    • Smooth falloff for τ < 1 and τ > 1
    • Linear modulation in q and pwm
    • A physically meaningful q×pwm interaction
    • Monotonicity in q (load) and pwm (switching duty)
The model is simple enough for embedded use but accurate enough for simulation.

